00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.550 align:start position:0%
so today i want to talk about five  things that i wished someone told me  before i started my phd so if you're  doing your phd right now you will  probably recognize these five things or  if you're thinking about doing your phd  these five things will probably help  guide you into making a better choice so  hi i'm shalotta fraser a second year phd  student in computational neuroscience at  the donors institute in the netherlands  right now  and today i want to talk about these  five things that i wish  someone told me before i started my phd  so the first thing is that the most  important choice you will make during  the start of your phd and what kind of  topic you want to do is probably the  choice of your supervisor so your  supervisor is the person that will  decide if you graduate and sign what  kind of papers you can publish what kind  of subjects you can research and also  what kind of money is allocated towards  your research so what kind of resources  you can get and that is why your  supervisor is the number one thing you  must pick right and i've seen before  that people choose based on the  university for example the university  name or based on the fame of some lab or  the location and i think all these  things are valid but what is more  important is that you have a good  supervisor so if your supervisor is  narcissistic or only wants his name on  the paper or has a very strong opinion  on what kind of research you can do you  will be very limited in your phd journey  whereas if your supervisor is  accommodating a very supportive and  allocates a lot of resources towards you  even if he is from a smaller lab your  phd experience and results that you get  from your phd are probably so much more  valuable and that's why i would really  recommend before starting your phd if  you can for example start a little  project with your supervisor so a little  lab rotation before you officially start  or if this is not possible to  for example interview some students that  have worked with him or her before such  that you get a really good insight into  what kind of person your supervisor is  and if for example your communication  skills match or if how you want to be  supervised matches with your supervisor  yeah and i think this will really make  your phd journey so much more smooth if  you have someone that matches you in  that sense because it is the person that  you will be most close with during your  phd journey and the second thing no one  really talks about is the importance of  writing papers and also how the paper  writing system is kind of broken so in  general your entire academic career will  be based on an age index and your age  index is basically correlated to how  many papers you published and how much  those papers got cited but of course  this system is quite easily to be rigged  and to be hacked so what big  universities for example do or big labs  do is they cite each other's papers such  that these labs have higher age indexes  and i think in general this is extremely  bad and almost everyone agrees with the  fact that the paper writing system is  broken in a sense but what no one really  talks about is the fact that you will  still be pushed as a phd student to  write in these big journals that are  known to be varied and that are known to  have faulty systems so if you are not  comfortable with playing this game i  would really recommend to look at  smaller laps that are less focused on  this kind of fame rigging but if you're  willing to play along a little bit just  keep in the back of your mind that this  is just part of being in academia and  hopefully it will change in the coming  years with the new generation and  another thing i think people don't talk  about enough as soon as you start your  phd you kind of have to think about what  you want to do after because i've seen a  lot of students that are graduating  right now that don't know for sure what  they want to do after and one of the  thing things is if you know beforehand  that you want to stay in academia you  really have to think about how to get  your funding after your phd for postdoc  and funding is based on for example how  many papers you wrote but also how many  teaching hours you had and if you know  this beforehand you can schedule your  phd in such a manner that you have some  teaching hours and also to choose  projects that are easily publishable but  if you know you want to go to industry  afterwards your phd journey would  probably look a little bit different i  would for example then recommend to do  some courses that are suitable for the  skills they ask in industry so for  example i know right now in industry  that i perhaps want to apply for they  ask java coding skills and i'm coding  right now in python but i know in my  final year of my phd i will probably  take a course in java coding and get  that up to speed such that i have a  smoother journey into applying for  certain industry jobs and the fourth  thing no one really talks about and  especially nowadays is that your social  circle during your phd matters so much  and i think your social circle is  everyone including the people you meet  in university but also the people  outside of university so for example  your family or your friends from before  university and i think people don't  really recognize enough how important  this support system will be during your  phd because there will be moments that  you're very tired or you feel very  lonely or you're not sure you want to  continue and this support system is  really the system that allows you to  keep strong and keep going so i see  around me that the phd students that do  the best are usually not the students  that are most motivated or that i have  really strong working ethic  although this is of course also really  important but it's usually the students  that actually have a really strong  support system that also really supports  their goals so for example they have  fellow phd students around them that can  help study and that can help motivate  each other to make those last few hours  before a paper deadline and the last  thing which i think is most important is  probably that the nature of your  research is such that nothing will  probably go exactly according to plan  and that is why you kind of need to plan  to feel early and fast at the beginning  because one of the most important things  you will learn during your phd is that  failure is a part of it and how to  handle failure will be probably one of  the things you will incorporate quite  quickly so that's why i would recommend  especially at the beginning of your phd  to not really focus on one project but  to have several projects and make them  kind of short but snappy and then when  you notice that one project is probably  has a lot of potential to focus on that  instead of putting all your eggs in one  basket so to say and something else that  i wish i had done a little bit more once  i started was to write up everything  even if an experiment failed because  i've seen before that papers were  published on things that i had done but  for them it worked but for me it failed  and i think it's really good to  sometimes get insight in exactly what  point you did differently or perhaps you  can write these researchers saying i did  something very similar this is my code  perhaps we can collaborate on the next  project so if you're struggling a little  bit with your phd i made another video  on some tips and tricks on how to  continue so to say and otherwise see you  next week  bye
 

